# Preserve Working Infrastructure

When adding features to existing files, you MUST preserve prior architectural contracts.

## Non-Negotiable Rules

1. **Check history before rewriting.** Before modifying positioning, layout, coordinate systems, or event handling in any component, run `git log --oneline -- <file>` and read fix commit messages. If a pattern was established by a fix commit, treat it as load-bearing.

2. **Extend, don't replace.** Add new props, callbacks, or CSS classes alongside existing ones. Never swap out a positioning model (e.g. JS-owned transform vs CSS-anchored) as a side-effect of adding a button or feature.

3. **One source of truth for position.** If a component's position is owned by JS state (e.g. `transform: translate(x, y)`), CSS must not also try to position it (no competing `top`/`right`/`left`). If CSS owns it, JS must not override it with transforms. Document which approach is used.

4. **Dynamic foundations under dynamic features.** If a visual element (grid, overlay, minimap) must track a dynamic value (zoom, pan, viewport size), its rendering must derive from that value — never use static CSS that ignores it.

5. **Document why, not what.** When a CSS rule uses `!important`, or a positioning approach is chosen, add a comment explaining the conflict it resolves. These comments are load-bearing — do not delete them during refactors.

## Examples

```css
/* ❌ BAD — static grid ignores zoom/pan, breaks when user zooms */
.workflow-canvas {
  background-size: 20px 20px;
}

/* ✅ GOOD — grid tracks zoom/pan via inline style */
/* CSS sets the default; JS overrides via style prop when zoom/pan change */
```

```typescript
// ❌ BAD — replacing JS-owned positioning with CSS-anchored as side-effect of adding a button
// v8 fix: JS owns position via transform. v10: silently replaced with CSS right:16px + JS top
// Result: positioning breaks again

// ✅ GOOD — add the button, keep the positioning model intact
// If you need to change the positioning model, do it in a dedicated commit with clear rationale
```
